## What I changed

**`diamond.h`** — declares the API:

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> rows(char letter);
```

**`diamond.cpp`** — builds the diamond:

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> rows(char letter) {
	const int n = letter - 'A';
	const int width = 2 * n + 1;
	std::vector<std::string> result;
	result.reserve(2 * n + 1);

	for (int i = 0; i <= 2 * n; ++i) {
		const int k = i <= n ? i : 2 * n - i;  // letter index for this row
		std::string row(width, ' ');
		row[n] = 'A' + k;
		if (k > 0) {
			row[n - k] = row[n + k] = 'A' + k;
		}
		result.push_back(std::move(row));
	}

	return result;
}
```

## How it works

For a target letter, `n = letter - 'A'` gives the diamond's half-width, so the square is `(2n+1) × (2n+1)`. For each row `i`, the letter index is `k = min(i, 2n − i)`, which makes the top half ascend (`A, B, …`) and the bottom half descend (`…, B, A`). Each row starts as all spaces; the center column always gets the letter, and when `k > 0` the letter is also placed at columns `n − k` and `n + k`, giving exactly `n − k` leading/trailing spaces and `2k − 1` interior spaces — matching all the expected outputs (including the full A–Z diamond).
